{
 "id": "2026-dou-colour",
 "name": "Colour, Shape and Area in Interior Space (SAM ratings)",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: the 24 room renderings from Figure 2 of Dou, Zhang, Zhang, Kang, Fan, Xie, Sun & Li (2026), Behavioral Sciences 16(8): 1364 (CC BY 4.0). The same 4.0 × 2.0 × 6.0 m room varies colour combination (contrasting red–green and yellow–purple vs similar red–orange and blue–purple), shape of the colour fields (angular: triangles, rectangles vs curved: circles, waves) and the proportion of surfaces coloured (25, 50, 100 %). Each room is shown for 10 seconds in three blocks of eight, followed by self-paced ratings of pleasure, arousal and dominance (Self-Assessment Manikin dimensions) and liking, all 1–9. Room ids in the data encode the design (e.g. r-g-sh-100), and the paper's cell means are embedded in the page. The paper found curved shapes buffered the displeasure of angular high-contrast rooms, and larger coloured areas raised arousal. Differences from the original study: (1) online still images instead of an immersive VR headset, and no EEG; (2) random order per participant instead of a Latin square; (3) SAM dimensions are asked in words with labelled 1–9 scales instead of the manikin pictures; (4) a self-report colour-vision question replaces the Ishihara test; (5) the renderings are crops of the published figure (542×238 px, soft).",
